#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "9ec1a675-6563-5374-85fd-871da49caf30" :question/math-academy-id "e-3727" :question/problem "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5,$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x = -\\sqrt 2$ and one local maximum at $x = \\sqrt 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/id #uuid "3f70f692-38d3-5b7e-9bbb-9fc089679fa0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3729" :question/problem "Find the stationary points of the function $f(x) =x^3$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t=3x^2,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a82def66-255f-5658-9109-ab7966855c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3730" :question/problem "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x+3){e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}=(2x+5){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+5){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =(2(-5)+5){e}^{-5}=-\\frac{5}{{e}^{5}}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(2(0)+5){e}^{0}=5>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left( -\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt $x=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3428605-2071-5dfd-af29-d6ef0c96c0af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103007} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d9d7a0-e9aa-5f50-8538-06afaaab65a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103008} {:answer/id #uuid "0d43707b-ee5a-57af-b124-47eb278a0791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103009} {:answer/id #uuid "27c5a236-3060-583a-9123-c978ad354f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103010} {:answer/id #uuid "606e69dd-42f4-5d0a-8bd3-3c8466a094df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d43707b-ee5a-57af-b124-47eb278a0791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103009} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f19366-c132-5008-bf78-90ea03a83a40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37b4a96f-ccf7-5874-8bcd-7955ee9c3b7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-22810" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ defined for $x\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$   on $(0,2\\pi )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points  $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\pi ,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2\\pi )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |  | $\\pi $ |  | $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd68adc4-d1bb-5ad7-b8a2-251cbb7c4158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd68adc4-d1bb-5ad7-b8a2-251cbb7c4158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293004} :answer-field/id #uuid "15a699aa-48fa-5e54-b0d7-946780762be2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62d1473d-f510-5d86-b244-10afe12d98ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-344262" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+27x+4$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+27.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+27=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 3.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =-3(-4)^{2}+27=-21<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0)^{2}+27=27>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-3(4)^{2}+27=-21<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3369ea3e-3a20-591e-8b28-8e30cae2ee49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3369ea3e-3a20-591e-8b28-8e30cae2ee49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293007} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5877b8-a4d7-5cb9-a4d6-9000754c3791" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "889709cf-f226-587b-b510-b013e95ddfc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344287" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+1$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-30x+24.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-30x+24=0\\,⟹\\,x=1,\\,4.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2,5$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =6(0)^{2}-30(0)+24=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2)^{2}-30(2)+24=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =6(5)^{2}-30(5)+24=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |  | $5$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=4,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=4$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=4.$"}]}
